  "HERED-NCERT-8-SA-001": { status: "publish", hash: "e166b6cea2d76f2d", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "HERED-NCERT-8-SA-002": { status: "publish", hash: "4df1180ff4631d93", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "HERED-NCERT-8-SA-005": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The key contradicts itself. It opens with 'No, NOT enough', but finalAnswer says 'Yes, A is dominant' and the steps conclude A is dominant. The NCERT answer is 'No, not enough information'. The blind answer says No, so it disagrees with the stored finalAnswer." },
  "HERED-NCERT-8-SA-006": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): The answer uses the term 'autosomes', which the chapter never uses. The chapter only says 22 pairs are matched pairs and one pair is the sex chromosomes. Rephrase as '22 pairs of ordinary chromosomes plus one pair of sex chromosomes'." },
  "HERED-NCERT-8-SA-007": { status: "publish", hash: "23e8ffd049e345c6", note: "book check MATCH, text faithful; syllabus IN (guard); blind solve agrees; judge + text/steps audit clean; second audit clean; third audit (review checklist) clean or kept by its written rule" },
  "HERED-NCERT-8-LA-001": { status: "hold", note: "judge/audit: The key says 'interbreed (or back-cross)' and expects a 3:1 ratio. A back-cross does not give 3:1 (a test cross gives 1:1). Remove the back-cross parenthetical, then it can publish." },
  "HERED-NCERT-8-SA-008": { status: "hold", note: "third audit (review checklist): The answer and steps teach 'meiosis', 'homologous chromosomes', 'somatic cells' and '2n/n' notation. None of these appear in the NCERT chapter text. The chapter says only that every germ cell takes one chromosome from each pair and that the combining of two germ cells restores the normal chromosome number. Rewrite using the chapter's own wording." },
